00:07:58,420 --> 00:08:04,560
PASSAGE 1
Pet dogs can often readily understand a wide range of their owners’ commands and gestures.

73
00:08:04,560 --> 00:08:10,440
But are these abilities inborn or are they exclusively learned through training?

74
00:08:10,440 --> 00:08:16,400
To find out, a new study examined whether stray dogs could understand human gestures.

75
00:08:16,400 --> 00:08:28,080
The study revealed that 80% of participating dogs successfully followed pointing gestures to a specific location despite having never received prior training.

76
00:08:28,080 --> 00:08:34,780
The results suggest that dogs can understand complex gestures by simply watching humans.

77
00:08:34,780 --> 00:08:41,360
This could have implications in reducing conflict between stray dogs and humans.

78
00:08:41,360 --> 00:08:46,140
Dogs were domesticated 10,000 to 15,000 years ago.

79
00:08:46,140 --> 00:08:51,680
This means they are probably the oldest domesticated animals on the planet.

80
00:08:51,680 --> 00:08:59,800
Humans then bred dogs with the most desirable and useful traits so that they could function as companions and workers.

81
00:08:59,800 --> 00:09:06,600
This resulted in domesticated dogs that are highly responsive to human commands and gestures.

82
00:09:06,600 --> 00:09:15,080
However, it was not clear whether dogs understand us through training alone, or whether this was an inherent ability.

83
00:09:15,080 --> 00:09:17,300
Why does this matter?

84
00:09:17,300 --> 00:09:20,940
Stray dogs are a common feature in cities around the world.

85
00:09:20,940 --> 00:09:30,000
They may observe and occasionally interact with people, but such dogs have never been trained and are behaviorally “wild”.

86
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:34,960
Conflicts between stray dogs and humans are a problem.

87
00:09:34,960 --> 00:09:41,500
Understanding how humans shape stray dogs’ behavior may help alleviate this problem.

88
00:09:41,500 --> 00:09:46,840
According to the researchers of the new study, stray dogs are intelligent creatures.

89
00:09:46,840 --> 00:09:52,920
They closely observe humans and use their understanding of us to make a decision.

90
00:09:52,920 --> 00:10:04,849
“Treating stray dogs with sensitivity and respect and giving them space,” the researchers assert, “can reduce much conflict.

91
00:10:04,849 --> 00:10:07,500
”

92
00:10:07,500 --> 00:10:28,360
Q9. What does the passage say about 80% of participating dogs in the new study?

93
00:10:28,360 --> 00:10:46,640
Q10. What may help alleviate the problem caused by stray dogs?

94
00:10:46,640 --> 00:11:07,460
Q11. What do the researchers suggest we do to reduce conflicts between stray dogs and humans?

95
00:11:07,460 --> 00:11:12,000
PASSAGE 2
Think your refrigerator is just one big box of evenly cold air?

96
00:11:12,000 --> 00:11:13,200
Not so!

97
00:11:13,200 --> 00:11:21,360
The temperature inside the average fridge actually varies from front to back, bottom to top and everywhere in between.

98
00:11:21,360 --> 00:11:28,920
Because of this, food product placement within the appliance is far more important than you probably realize.

99
00:11:28,920 --> 00:11:36,940
The whole purpose of the modern refrigerator is to slow the growth of bacteria, which thrive in temperatures from 4.

100
00:11:36,940 --> 00:11:40,460
4 to 60 °C.

101
00:11:40,460 --> 00:11:49,300
As a result, refrigerators are typically set at or below that low end to protect foods against bacteria.

102
00:11:49,300 --> 00:12:01,760
Although it might seem like time spent organizing the fridge will spoil your day, doing so will actually make it more user-friendly, easier to clean and food safety-conscious.

103
00:12:01,760 --> 00:12:13,340
Those little bins on the inside of refrigerator doors are convenient, but they don’t maintain a consistent temperature, so definitely refrain from putting perishable foods in there.

104
00:12:13,340 --> 00:12:21,720
The top area is not as cold as the bottom of the fridge, so save it for the foods that don’t need extra-chilly temperatures.

105
00:12:21,720 --> 00:12:26,820
Store foods that are ready-to-eat or already cooked on the top shelf.

106
00:12:26,820 --> 00:12:33,280
The middle shelf is the most temperature-controlled part of the fridge, so place eggs here.

107
00:12:33,280 --> 00:12:40,020
Drinks and prepared foods can also live on the middle shelf if you run out of room up top.

108
00:12:40,020 --> 00:12:49,100
The back of the bottom shelf is the coldest spot in the fridge, so store perishable dairy products here to keep them edible, longer.

109
00:12:49,100 --> 00:13:04,000
Never store foods with high water content here, because the water within them can and will freeze, effectively ruining them.

110
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:23,600
Q12. Why is food product placement within the fridge very important?

111
00:13:23,600 --> 00:13:42,700
Q13. What does the passage say is the whole purpose of the modern refrigerator?

112
00:13:42,700 --> 00:14:01,060
Q14. Where in the fridge should we store foods already cooked?

113
00:14:01,060 --> 00:14:47,260
Q15. Why should we never store foods with high water content at the back of the bottom shelf?

114
00:14:47,260 --> 00:14:54,380
RECORDING 1
My current research is mainly looking at factors like stress and how would it affect our immune system.

115
00:14:54,380 --> 00:15:01,680
So the current projects that I am quite excited about are looking at older people and factors that are important to them.

116
00:15:01,680 --> 00:15:10,800
In one project, we are looking at hip fracture in older people, which is a common event, and we are trying to understand the impact of depression.

117
00:15:10,800 --> 00:15:21,360
Often people in hospital with a hip fracture develop depression, and these are the people that then go on to become feeble a lot more quickly than others.

118
00:15:21,360 --> 00:15:25,840
They get infections; often, these infections lead to death.

119
00:15:25,840 --> 00:15:30,040
So we’re trying to slow down that progression to feebleness.

120
00:15:30,040 --> 00:15:37,220
So if we can better understand how these factors interact, then we can actually start doing something about it.

121
00:15:37,220 --> 00:15:49,320
The impact that my research will have in Birmingham, first of all, is obviously for older people, because these are the people that are having hip fractures and developing depression.

122
00:15:49,320 --> 00:15:53,640
And we’re trying to improve their health and their quality of life.

123
00:15:53,640 --> 00:15:58,440
But this isn’t just a problem in Birmingham or even in the UK.

124
00:15:58,440 --> 00:15:59,940
This is a problem globally.

125
00:15:59,940 --> 00:16:02,320
People are aging.

126
00:16:02,320 --> 00:16:11,320
There’re a lot more people in the older generation who are living for longer, but not necessarily staying healthy for a large part of their lives.

127
00:16:11,320 --> 00:16:21,940
So the last part of their life can often be full of ill health and we’re trying to understand factors that can contribute to the quality of life later on.

128
00:16:21,940 --> 00:16:27,620
I think the thing that excites me most about my research is that it feels real and relevant.

129
00:16:27,620 --> 00:16:36,080
Lots of academics are probably very excited about their research, but it might not be that relevant to those around them.

130
00:16:36,080 --> 00:16:42,120
Whereas I feel that the work I do is relevant to everyone, because everyone is aging.

131
00:16:42,120 --> 00:16:45,520
Health is an issue for everyone, regardless of their age.

132
00:16:45,520 --> 00:16:53,360
And what I’m trying to do is understand the factors that affect health and how we can improve the quality of life and people.

133
00:16:53,360 --> 00:16:57,980
So that’s one of the things that really excites me about it.

134
00:16:57,980 --> 00:17:25,360
One of the other things is rather than just taking one perspective — so rather than looking at aging and health just from the perspective of the psychologist — we are now bringing together a lot of different techniques and a lot of different viewpoints to actually look at the sort of different factors affecting health.

135
00:17:25,360 --> 00:17:43,820
Q16. What does the speaker say is quite common among older people?

136
00:17:43,820 --> 00:18:02,060
Q17. What does the speaker think excites her most about her research?

137
00:18:02,060 --> 00:18:20,100
Q18. How does the speaker approach her research project?

138
00:18:20,100 --> 00:18:22,960
RECORDING 2
Americans are obsessed with celebrity chefs.

139
00:18:22,960 --> 00:18:26,900
We talk about them and try to eat like them.

140
00:18:26,900 --> 00:18:32,120
But could we learn something more from them than recipes and techniques?

141
00:18:32,120 --> 00:18:41,160
According to Marketdata Enterprises, Americans spend nearly $10 billion a year on self-help and personal organization products.

142
00:18:41,160 --> 00:18:48,780
The market is huge, partly because most colleges and graduate schools don’t teach basic organization.

143
00:18:48,780 --> 00:18:52,580
But cooking schools and professional kitchens do.

144
00:18:52,580 --> 00:19:00,840
Perhaps the principles of cooking organization can be extended to help even those of us who aren’t top chefs.

145
00:19:00,840 --> 00:19:07,420
The system that makes kitchens function is called mise-en-place, or, literally, “put in place”.

146
00:19:07,420 --> 00:19:13,800
It’s a French phrase that means to gather and arrange the ingredients and tools needed for cooking.

147
00:19:13,800 --> 00:19:17,940
But for many professional cooks, the phrase means something deeper.

148
00:19:17,940 --> 00:19:20,220
Some cooks call it their religion.

149
00:19:20,220 --> 00:19:29,860
It helps them coordinate vast amounts of labor and material, and transforms the lives of its practitioners through focus and self-discipline.

150
00:19:29,860 --> 00:19:34,580
Students at the Culinary Institute of America view it as a way of life.

151
00:19:34,580 --> 00:19:44,140
It’s a way of concentrating their mind to only focus on the aspects that they need to be working on at the moment, to kind of rid themselves of distractions.

152
00:19:44,140 --> 00:19:48,740
And it’s a habit that some students carry with them even when they’re not in the kitchen.

153
00:19:48,740 --> 00:19:51,380
They mise-en-place their life.

154
00:19:51,380 --> 00:20:00,780
They set up their books for class, they set up their chef whites, they shine their shoes, they know everything that they need every step of the day.

155
00:20:00,780 --> 00:20:04,780
Then how does one attain this level of organization?

156
00:20:04,780 --> 00:20:09,100
The key to high level organization is the mindset.

157
00:20:09,100 --> 00:20:13,680
Cooks can easily do 6 hours of preparation for a 3-hour dinner shift.

158
00:20:13,680 --> 00:20:21,460
Mise-en-place forces cooks to account for every minute of their time and every movement.

159
00:20:21,460 --> 00:20:33,880
“Every component of one single dish is in one single corner so their hand literally moves inches,” explains Dwayne Lipuma, an instructor at the Culinary Institute of America.

160
00:20:33,880 --> 00:20:38,180
“Once students set up their station, I should be able to cover up their eyes.

161
00:20:38,180 --> 00:20:45,534
And they should know that their knives are always here, their oil is always right here, their salt and pepper is always right here.

162
00:20:45,534 --> 00:20:46,760
”

163
00:20:46,760 --> 00:20:52,780
But practiced at its highest level, mise-en-place says that time is precious.

164
00:20:52,780 --> 00:20:54,340
Resources are precious.

165
00:20:54,340 --> 00:20:56,360
Space is precious.

166
00:20:56,360 --> 00:21:00,260
Your self-respect and the respect of others are precious.

167
00:21:00,260 --> 00:21:02,160
Use them wisely.

168
00:21:02,160 --> 00:21:12,920
Isn’t that a philosophy for our time?

169
00:21:12,920 --> 00:21:32,460
Q19. What does the speaker say about most colleges and graduate schools in America?

170
00:21:32,460 --> 00:21:53,780
Q20. How do students at the Culinary Institute of America view mise-en-place or “put in place”?

171
00:21:53,780 --> 00:22:12,640
Q21. What does the speaker advise us to do at the end of the talk?

172
00:22:12,640 --> 00:22:18,500
RECORDING 3
Why do we stop making goals for ourselves and stop progressing in life?

173
00:22:18,500 --> 00:22:25,400
One reason is the belief that we should accept who we are and not constantly strive to improve ourselves.

174
00:22:25,400 --> 00:22:34,360
The mistake we make with this is that whilst self-acceptance is imperative, it should actually be used as the basis for growth.

175
00:22:34,360 --> 00:22:45,100
When we are not growing, learning and seeking improvements in our lives, we tend to stagnate, accepting what it is and stop believing we can be better.

176
00:22:45,100 --> 00:22:51,200
A key tool for creating goals and progressing is using our imagination.

177
00:22:51,200 --> 00:23:02,720
Yet as is so often the case, by the time we’ve reached midlife, we’ve stopped dreaming about what we want to achieve and feel quite negatively about goal-setting.

178
00:23:02,720 --> 00:23:11,560
Instead of feeling confidence, happiness and satisfaction from achieving our goals, we feel we are always falling short.

179
00:23:11,560 --> 00:23:15,680
That leads to disappointment and we stop dreaming.

180
00:23:15,680 --> 00:23:26,040
We stop imagining the life we’d like to have or the person we’d like to be, quickly finding ourselves shattered by the pain of our reality.

181
00:23:26,040 --> 00:23:34,380
Up until now, unless we work in the creative industry, we’ve found the idea of using “play” a bit childish.

182
00:23:34,380 --> 00:23:48,700
In the West, we are mostly operating from our heads, the rational mind, but it’s beginning to be better understood, because using our imagination is actually one of the most powerful things we could ever do.

183
00:23:48,700 --> 00:23:58,000
The problem with finding ourselves irritated for not achieving the goal is because the goal always changes and we’re always adapting, whether we realize it or not.

184
00:23:58,000 --> 00:24:04,600
What happens, therefore, is that we end up feeling like we are never going to reach our goals.

185
00:24:04,600 --> 00:24:12,280
And herein lies one of the biggest misconceptions and reasons for failure, dissatisfaction, and unhappiness.

186
00:24:12,280 --> 00:24:16,860
We measure against the goal rather than where we’ve come from.

187
00:24:16,860 --> 00:24:20,640
Dan Sullivan calls this “the gap and the gain”.

188
00:24:20,640 --> 00:24:27,000
We measure always against the goal, which we never feel we’re reaching, rather than the gain.

189
00:24:27,000 --> 00:24:29,520
That is where we’ve come from.

190
00:24:29,520 --> 00:24:35,720
In measuring where we’ve come from, rather than where we’re trying to get to, we see results.

191
00:24:35,720 --> 00:24:39,620
We see things we’ve taken for granted or not noticed.

192
00:24:39,620 --> 00:24:49,240
It’s a simple, yet profound change in perception, and what I love about it is the power it has to instantly shift us up a gear.

193
00:24:49,240 --> 00:25:03,460
It’s so, so simple, yet has an immediate, positive impact on our thinking.

194
00:25:03,460 --> 00:25:22,460
Q22. What should self-acceptance be used as according to the speaker?

195
00:25:22,460 --> 00:25:40,340
Q23. What do people tend to do when they have reached midlife?

196
00:25:40,340 --> 00:25:59,060
Q24. Why do we find ourselves irritated for not achieving the goal?

197
00:25:59,060 --> 00:26:20,669
Q25. What does the speaker say has an immediate, positive impact on our thinking?
